<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Culavamsa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chulavamsa, Cūḷavaṃsa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Culavamsa is a Pali chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> continuing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a>, compiled in stages by Buddhist monks from the 13th century onwards. It covers the island's history from the 4th century CE to the British conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> in 1815, recording the deeds of kings, their wars with south Indian powers and their patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name means Lesser Chronicle. The work is generally considered a sequel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a>, the Great Chronicle written in the 6th century by the monk Mahanama, and the two are sometimes treated as a single work spanning over two millennia of Sri Lankan history. Unlike the Mahavamsa, the Cūḷavaṃsa was written by different authors at different periods. Tradition ascribes its first part to the Buddhist monk Thera Dhammakitti, though many historians now believe it to be the collaborative work of several monks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The chronicle falls into two parts. The first, chapters thirty-seven to seventy-nine, written in the 13th century, begins with the arrival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> of a tooth relic of Siddhartha Gautama and runs to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parakramabahu-i">Parakramabahu the Great</a> (1153–1186). The second, by an unknown author, continues from Vijayabahu II to Parakramabahu IV (chapters 80–90). Within it the section from Parakramabahu IV to the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirti-sri-rajasinha">Kirti Sri Rajasinha</a> was compiled by Tibbotuvāve Sumangala Thera, and the last chapter was carried to 1815 by Hikkaduve Siri Sumangala. These later portions are generally considered inferior to Dhammakitti's in style and in factual reliability.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Wilhelm Geiger, who translated it from Pali into German, saw the history of Parakramabahu as the real kernel of the work, as the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutugemunu">Dutugemunu</a> is of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a>. In his view the compiler drew from his reading an ideal picture of an Indian king and fitted the king to it, without asking whether the details were probable. Geiger held that the chronicle does not consciously relate false information but exaggerates and embellishes, and that its truth can be recovered with caution. Carvings, edicts and monuments confirm parts of it, such as Parakramabahu's wars with Gajabahu II and Manabharana, and it is the primary source for his reign.</p><p>In 1871 the British colonial government commissioned a 101st chapter covering 1815 to 1871. In 1935 the monk Yagirala Pannananda wrote Mahavamsa Part III, a further extension in Sinhala, and in 1977 the government of Prime Minister JR Jayawardene set up a commission to extend the chronicle to cover 1935 to 1977.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cūḷavaṃsa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1284 a Pandya army under the commander Aryacakravarti invaded Sri Lanka, took the fortress of Yapahuwa and carried off the Tooth Relic of the Buddha, which King Parakramabahu III later recovered by negotiation, according to the <b>Culavamsa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
